    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>It tells of the creation of the world and of humans, who after failed attempts with earth and with wood were made from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and of the Hero Twins who defeat the lords of the underworld, Xibalba.</li><li>Its only known text is the parallel Kʼicheʼ and Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> Francisco Ximénez, taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 1850s and now in the Newberry Library in Chicago.</li><li>Scenes from its stories appear on Maya ceramics, it remains part of the beliefs of many Kʼicheʼ, and in 2012 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> declared it intangible cultural heritage.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The planet Camazotz in Madeleine L'Engle's A Wrinkle in Time (1962) is named for the bat-god of the Popol Vuh's story of the Hero Twins.</p></aside>
    </section>

